00:02:36.893 --> 00:02:39.398
Welcome to this session about
accessibility ownership.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.968
Before I get started, just a couple
of words why I'm, why I'm here.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.154
So my name is Sanna Kramsi, like
I said, and I work as a senior

00:02:47.155 --> 00:02:50.107
accessibility engineer, developer,
and competence manager at a Finnish

00:02:50.111 --> 00:02:51.472
digital agency called Exove.

00:02:51.826 --> 00:02:55.704
And I also maintain my own accessibility
content under A11y-ing with Sanna.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.654
I'm IAAP certified professional
in web accessibility, and you can

00:02:59.655 --> 00:03:02.670
find my content at A-1-1-Y dot ing.

00:03:02.785 --> 00:03:06.017
It's a wordplay, A11y-ing, and
you can find me also on LinkedIn.

00:03:06.847 --> 00:03:10.105
Now, I've been working with different
size projects for over fifteen

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:13.668
years, and I've seen firsthand how
good intentions just aren't enough.

00:03:14.343 --> 00:03:17.195
Without good processes, the level
of accessibility will start to

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:20.904
decline, and I know that sounds bad,
but that's unfortunately the truth.

00:03:21.524 --> 00:03:24.911
Because sometimes people think that
because the site was originally

00:03:24.912 --> 00:03:28.198
built accessible, that it just
keeps on being accessible no matter

00:03:28.198 --> 00:03:29.784
what happens to it after launch.

00:03:29.984 --> 00:03:32.522
I've heard something like this
so many times that I think

00:03:32.522 --> 00:03:33.691
we need to tackle this myth.

00:03:34.608 --> 00:03:38.327
So on this slide, I have a timeline
of a quite common type of a project.

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:43.268
It starts with definition, then design,
development, content input, and launch.

00:03:43.902 --> 00:03:47.510
We all know that site launch is just
one point during its life cycle.

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:48.606
It doesn't end there.

00:03:48.849 --> 00:03:51.911
It's very rare that a site would
remain identical after it launches.

00:03:52.529 --> 00:03:56.036
There's usually continuous development
containing server updates, plugin

00:03:56.053 --> 00:03:58.825
updates, feature development, and
also new and updating content.

00:03:59.251 --> 00:04:02.158
And if we think none of these will
have an impact on accessibility,

00:04:02.212 --> 00:04:03.743
we're in for a rough reality check.

00:04:04.971 --> 00:04:08.000
It's important to remember that
accessibility is not something that

00:04:08.002 --> 00:04:09.805
is done once and then forgotten.

00:04:10.322 --> 00:04:13.786
Accessibility is constantly
affected when we touch a website.

00:04:14.346 --> 00:04:16.488
And I want to highlight one point.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.543
Accessibility doesn't usually
fail because we don't care.

00:04:20.818 --> 00:04:23.469
It fails because ownership is unclear.

00:04:23.986 --> 00:04:27.461
It's easy to think that if people
just cared, things would be right.

00:04:28.076 --> 00:04:31.734
And while caring does matter, in
reality of the business world, it

00:04:31.754 --> 00:04:33.419
doesn't really get you all that far.

00:04:34.108 --> 00:04:37.653
Accessibility needs processes,
and processes need ownership

00:04:39.103 --> 00:04:41.627
So let's talk about where
WordPress accessibility breaks.

00:04:42.105 --> 00:04:45.545
And of course, most of these apply
to any website, but WordPress

00:04:45.564 --> 00:04:48.497
has a few characteristics that
make this especially relevant.

00:04:49.142 --> 00:04:52.039
We know that first of all, it's super
popular, so there's all sorts of

00:04:52.044 --> 00:04:55.889
websites built with it, and WordPress
often gives content editors considerable

00:04:55.889 --> 00:04:57.768
freedom over content and presentation.

00:04:58.686 --> 00:05:01.851
And this, unfortunately, also means
that on a WordPress site, accessibility

00:05:01.876 --> 00:05:03.853
ownership becomes even more crucial.

00:05:05.535 --> 00:05:08.170
Let's start by taking a look
at the website ecosystem.

00:05:08.268 --> 00:05:11.257
Obviously, I don't need to tell you
about how WordPress works, but the

00:05:11.258 --> 00:05:14.641
important thing here is that when we
consider the people responsible for

00:05:14.643 --> 00:05:18.101
a website, we have many different
roles and levels of expertise.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.295
And in some cases, there might be
several people in the same role

00:05:22.357 --> 00:05:24.259
or people with more than one role.

00:05:24.906 --> 00:05:27.887
And no matter the role, there are
usually juniors and seniors alike.

00:05:29.075 --> 00:05:33.365
This slide shows a simplified map of the
roles and areas involved in a website.

00:05:33.626 --> 00:05:36.726
Website is in the middle with
all the roles pointing to it.

00:05:37.417 --> 00:05:41.819
We have tasks that relate to sysadmin
work, design, development, SEO, testing,

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.639
marketing, content, and so much more.

00:05:44.362 --> 00:05:48.145
These depend on the site in question,
but legal might define obligations

00:05:48.873 --> 00:05:51.819
and data analytics teams might
introduce tracking and content tools

00:05:51.819 --> 00:05:53.513
that affect the user experience.

00:05:54.023 --> 00:05:55.957
So where does accessibility come in?

00:05:56.623 --> 00:05:59.962
Well, to be honest, I think we
could replace the website in

00:05:59.963 --> 00:06:01.527
this graphic with accessibility.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.148
All the other roles relate to it.

00:06:04.958 --> 00:06:09.228
Accessibility is not its own separate area
when it comes to website accessibility.

00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:14.851
There can be people whose job it
is to do accessibility work, but

00:06:14.926 --> 00:06:18.298
accessibility needs to be considered
in every change we make to the site.

00:06:19.709 --> 00:06:22.682
And when talking about these roles,
in some cases, all of these roles

00:06:22.686 --> 00:06:26.161
are within a single organization
and communication flows more easily.

00:06:26.541 --> 00:06:29.444
Issues might be solved by a simple
phone call or a chat message.

00:06:29.824 --> 00:06:34.080
But when there's one or more external
partners, it can get complicated.

00:06:34.905 --> 00:06:38.444
When design belongs to one agency,
development to another, and the content

00:06:38.488 --> 00:06:42.884
to the client, the communication can turn
into legal contracts and siloed issues.

00:06:43.652 --> 00:06:47.035
And sometimes information doesn't
flow from one agency to another, and,

00:06:47.037 --> 00:06:48.428
well, you can guess where that leads.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:53.849
So the thing is that if accessibility
lacks ownership or it is considered

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:57.764
a completely separate thing that is
not related to all the other areas,

00:06:58.187 --> 00:06:59.592
there will eventually be chaos.

00:07:00.608 --> 00:07:03.828
Let's talk about a few post-launch
activities that might cause issues for us

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:10.181
In my experience, accessibility of, of
content is one of the first places where

00:07:10.227 --> 00:07:13.709
post-launch ownership gaps become visible.

00:07:14.202 --> 00:07:17.456
Content accessibility depends
too much on individual knowledge.

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.964
WordPress gives content editors
a great deal of freedom.

00:07:21.351 --> 00:07:24.918
They can affect headings, content
order, colors, links, images,

00:07:24.948 --> 00:07:29.621
videos, downloadable documents, and
that flexibility is very valuable,

00:07:29.621 --> 00:07:33.410
but it also means that editors can
significantly affect accessibility.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:38.668
And the problem is that editors are often
left without enough guidance or support.

00:07:39.597 --> 00:07:43.271
Accessibility then depends on what
each editor happens to know and

00:07:43.271 --> 00:07:48.041
remember, and their priorities might
also be decided by someone else, and

00:07:48.041 --> 00:07:51.759
accessibility might lose to a more
urgent marketing or publishing request.

00:07:52.697 --> 00:07:55.494
Clear responsibilities tell
editors what they're expected

00:07:55.494 --> 00:07:56.963
to check before publishing.

00:07:57.715 --> 00:08:01.102
Accessible defaults also reduce
what they need to remember.

00:08:01.344 --> 00:08:04.583
Approved blocks, templates, color
combinations, and publishing

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:07.759
settings can prevent common
problems before they happen.

00:08:08.794 --> 00:08:11.357
Without these, the results are familiar.

00:08:12.273 --> 00:08:16.028
Unclear headings and links, missing
alt text, inaccessible media,

00:08:16.068 --> 00:08:21.696
problematic PDF files, but none of those
individual mistakes are the main issue.

00:08:22.368 --> 00:08:26.376
The main issue is that the organization
has made accessibility dependent

00:08:26.418 --> 00:08:30.043
on individual knowledge instead
of supporting it through clear

00:08:30.044 --> 00:08:32.354
expectations and publishing processes.

00:08:34.328 --> 00:08:40.013
Then, of course, we have the
code-level issues, because updates

00:08:40.096 --> 00:08:41.819
can also introduce regressions.

00:08:42.308 --> 00:08:46.918
A plugin, theme, or WordPress core
update might change markup, styles,

00:08:46.955 --> 00:08:51.571
or interaction behavior even when no
one intentionally changes the feature.

00:08:52.793 --> 00:08:56.531
Now, when a client or a site owner
requests a new plugin, they don't often

00:08:56.532 --> 00:09:00.026
know anything about its quality, and
not that they necessarily need to.

00:09:00.032 --> 00:09:01.415
It's not really their job to.

00:09:02.203 --> 00:09:06.483
But then the plugin might meet the
business need but still be inaccessible.

00:09:07.461 --> 00:09:09.943
And the same, of course, applies
to any new feature requests.

00:09:11.396 --> 00:09:15.772
So developers need enough accessibility
knowledge to evaluate semantic

00:09:15.797 --> 00:09:21.149
HTML, custom interactions, ARIA, and
relevant assistive technology behavior.

00:09:21.744 --> 00:09:24.930
Without that knowledge, regressions
and third-party problems

00:09:24.966 --> 00:09:26.956
are much harder to recognize

00:09:29.918 --> 00:09:32.563
Handovers are another
common breaking point.

00:09:33.088 --> 00:09:36.779
I've encountered several successful
projects where a project is

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:40.026
finished, and then it's up to
the client, and that's normal.

00:09:40.686 --> 00:09:44.354
But sometimes no real documentation
was provided, and the client

00:09:44.355 --> 00:09:45.853
is left to fend for themselves.

00:09:46.524 --> 00:09:50.334
It really isn't a surprise that often
accessibility will start degrading,

00:09:51.311 --> 00:09:55.112
and this places a lot of extra
responsibility on the client's shoulders.

00:09:56.425 --> 00:10:00.221
The organization operating the service
is usually ultimately accountable

00:10:00.225 --> 00:10:03.134
for its accessibility even when
implementation is outsourced.

00:10:03.676 --> 00:10:07.816
But leaving that organization without
technical documentation, training,

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.148
or a clear transition process is
just poor practice, in my opinion.

00:10:14.018 --> 00:10:17.848
And when we talk about handovers
in a multi-agency environment,

00:10:18.374 --> 00:10:22.406
the poor handovers become more
common and more problematic.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:26.544
So in a case where we have a design
agency, development agency, and the

00:10:26.545 --> 00:10:31.424
client working together, it's crucial
that the information flows and proper

00:10:31.426 --> 00:10:35.591
handovers are done whenever another
party is expected to do something.

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:39.785
So what's behind all these problems?

00:10:40.629 --> 00:10:45.297
We often make the mistake of focusing
too much on the single problems and

00:10:45.311 --> 00:10:47.164
don't take a look at the big picture.

00:10:48.049 --> 00:10:52.616
But usually when we do that, we notice
that we lack communication, clear

00:10:52.616 --> 00:10:58.121
boundaries, and ownership, and without
these, everyone assumes the famous

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.957
someone else did it, and then no one does

00:11:03.877 --> 00:11:07.690
We've now seen several places where
accessibility can break after launch,

00:11:08.319 --> 00:11:10.310
and behind them is the same question.

00:11:11.113 --> 00:11:14.407
Who makes sure that
somebody actually acts?

00:11:15.308 --> 00:11:17.128
And that brings us to ownership.

00:11:19.139 --> 00:11:23.065
Now, before looking at individual roles,
I want to define the terms I'll use today.

00:11:23.742 --> 00:11:28.865
In this talk, ownership is the overall
system made up of accountability,

00:11:28.984 --> 00:11:30.998
responsibility, and coordination.

00:11:31.871 --> 00:11:36.105
The organization operating the service
usually holds overall accountability.

00:11:37.476 --> 00:11:41.482
Designers, developers, content
editors, managers, external

00:11:41.482 --> 00:11:45.487
partners are responsible for
accessibility within their own work.

00:11:46.439 --> 00:11:51.441
And to avoid confusing that work with
overall accountability, I'll call

00:11:51.442 --> 00:11:55.384
the person or group that coordinates
the system the accessibility lead.

00:11:56.058 --> 00:11:59.099
This is a functional label,
not necessarily a job title.

00:12:00.157 --> 00:12:05.390
The accessibility lead keeps priorities,
decisions, and follow-up connected, but

00:12:05.402 --> 00:12:07.566
doesn't personally perform every task.

00:12:09.366 --> 00:12:10.608
Let's use a small example.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:14.716
Imagine that someone requests
a new plugin for the site.

00:12:15.562 --> 00:12:19.785
The organization operating the service
remains accountable for the outcome.

00:12:20.656 --> 00:12:24.410
The development or maintenance team
might be responsible for evaluating

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:25.594
and implementing the plugin.

00:12:26.595 --> 00:12:30.770
The accessibility lead coordinates the
process by making sure the requirements

00:12:30.771 --> 00:12:35.079
are clear, the right people perform
the checks, and an authorized person

00:12:35.134 --> 00:12:37.232
makes the decision based on the results.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:42.938
These are three different functions,
and all of them are necessary

00:12:45.909 --> 00:12:49.213
Let's take a couple of examples
of accessibility responsibilities

00:12:49.229 --> 00:12:53.306
by role or job title, and this is
by far not a comprehensive list.

00:12:54.376 --> 00:12:58.572
But we might have project managers
who help ensure accessibility work has

00:12:58.593 --> 00:13:03.276
time and budget, and that expectations
remain clear during handovers.

00:13:05.013 --> 00:13:09.357
Then we have designers with the
responsibility of ensuring new feature

00:13:09.357 --> 00:13:13.563
requests or layouts, maintain color
contrasts, focus dates, and so on

00:13:13.582 --> 00:13:15.667
before the handoff to developers.

00:13:17.002 --> 00:13:21.395
And then developers are responsible for
things like semantic HTML and ensuring

00:13:21.398 --> 00:13:24.953
that theme updates don't override
any accessibility customizations.

00:13:25.918 --> 00:13:28.123
And they should also be
checking plugin outputs.

00:13:29.861 --> 00:13:33.462
Then we have content editors who are
responsible for the content, whether

00:13:33.462 --> 00:13:35.689
it's text, images, videos, and so on.

00:13:35.689 --> 00:13:39.174
So image alt text, descriptive
link text, and proper heading

00:13:39.174 --> 00:13:41.120
structures are their responsibility.

00:13:42.784 --> 00:13:47.252
And then we have the site owner,
and here by site owner, I mean the

00:13:47.254 --> 00:13:51.560
organization or role responsible for
the service, not necessarily the person

00:13:51.575 --> 00:13:53.263
maintaining WordPress day to day.

00:13:55.097 --> 00:13:59.001
The accessibility lead and site
owner might be the same person,

00:13:59.509 --> 00:14:00.535
but they don't have to be.

00:14:01.407 --> 00:14:05.094
The site owner holds overall
accountability, priorities,

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:08.891
and risk acceptance, and the
accessibility lead keeps the work

00:14:08.901 --> 00:14:11.182
connected across roles and partners.

00:14:13.491 --> 00:14:17.653
There are many roles and
responsibilities, and all of them matter.

00:14:18.476 --> 00:14:22.325
We often remember designers and
developers while content editors

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:23.967
are very easily overlooked.

00:14:24.958 --> 00:14:28.913
Because so many roles affect
accessibility, someone needs to keep

00:14:28.913 --> 00:14:33.305
the whole picture connected, and
that is the accessibility lead's role

00:14:35.709 --> 00:14:38.971
So what happens when we have
multiple agencies involved?

00:14:39.534 --> 00:14:44.031
In my experience, the result can often
become blame game, a bit chaotic.

00:14:44.828 --> 00:14:49.154
Not because the agencies are bad at
their jobs, but because no one agreed

00:14:49.166 --> 00:14:52.992
who would coordinate accessibility,
who would make decisions, and what

00:14:53.068 --> 00:14:55.251
each organization was responsible for.

00:14:56.358 --> 00:15:00.675
The scope might be siloed, with each
organization pointing to what was

00:15:00.728 --> 00:15:03.279
or wasn't included in its contract.

00:15:04.045 --> 00:15:07.005
The original project team might
disappear after launch, while

00:15:07.029 --> 00:15:10.332
maintenance then inherits code
and decisions it didn't make.

00:15:11.565 --> 00:15:15.228
And the delivery team might assume
that accessibility became the

00:15:15.231 --> 00:15:18.650
client's responsibility at the
handover while the client assumes the

00:15:18.651 --> 00:15:20.108
maintenance team will take care of it.

00:15:21.117 --> 00:15:24.834
We could talk separate hour about
the problem asss- of assumptions,

00:15:24.853 --> 00:15:26.505
but let's not go there today.

00:15:27.603 --> 00:15:31.548
But this thing is where things
get messy in the real world.

00:15:32.036 --> 00:15:36.218
When multiple vendors are involved,
accessibility often becomes a hot potato

00:15:36.252 --> 00:15:37.930
that n- no one really wants to touch.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:42.766
The design agency claims they handed over
a beautiful and accessible design concept,

00:15:43.142 --> 00:15:46.901
and the development agency claims they
built exactly what was in the Figma files.

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:51.622
The client assumes the site is
bulletproof because they paid both

00:15:51.697 --> 00:15:53.488
agencies thousands of dollars.

00:15:55.132 --> 00:15:58.310
Let's make that earlier plugin
request example a bit more concrete.

00:15:58.894 --> 00:16:02.305
So let's say three months after
launch, marketing requests a new

00:16:02.314 --> 00:16:04.226
form plugin and needs it quickly.

00:16:05.122 --> 00:16:07.790
The client assumes the maintenance
team will check accessibility.

00:16:08.382 --> 00:16:11.236
The maintenance team sees plugin
selection as a client decision.

00:16:11.641 --> 00:16:14.593
And then the original design agency
is no longer involved in this process.

00:16:15.661 --> 00:16:19.567
Let's keep this form plugin as our
example throughout this talk because

00:16:19.601 --> 00:16:23.868
it shows how the lightweight practices
I'll introduce soon can work together.

00:16:25.178 --> 00:16:28.983
And of course, it's also still good
to remember that the organization

00:16:29.028 --> 00:16:32.704
operating the service is usually
ultimately accountable for its

00:16:32.704 --> 00:16:36.812
accessibility, even when implementation
and even maintenance are outsourced.

00:16:37.555 --> 00:16:40.156
But I don't think they
have to do it all alone.

00:16:41.482 --> 00:16:43.930
Why does accessibility need a named lead?

00:16:45.343 --> 00:16:48.698
Everyone has accessibility
responsibilities in their own area,

00:16:49.494 --> 00:16:52.465
but distributed responsibility
is not enough on its own.

00:16:53.366 --> 00:16:57.165
Someone still needs to keep
priorities, decisions, processes,

00:16:57.263 --> 00:16:59.164
monitoring, and goals connected.

00:16:59.915 --> 00:17:02.261
That is what the accessibility lead does.

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:05.862
So the lead doesn't perform
every task personally.

00:17:06.366 --> 00:17:11.326
They maintain oversight and make sure
the different parts of the work connect.

00:17:11.945 --> 00:17:18.009
But to do that effectively, they also need
a mandate, enough authority to influence

00:17:18.117 --> 00:17:24.459
decisions, priorities, and processes
So who can be the accessibility lead?

00:17:25.749 --> 00:17:28.769
So the accessibility lead keeps
oversight of the whole process.

00:17:28.906 --> 00:17:32.443
So once again, they don't need to perform
every accessibility check, make every

00:17:32.443 --> 00:17:34.976
fix, or personally manage every task.

00:17:35.507 --> 00:17:37.117
I might say this a couple of times today.

00:17:38.216 --> 00:17:43.043
The individual tasks need named
responsible people in design, development,

00:17:43.148 --> 00:17:45.163
content, testing, and maintenance.

00:17:46.081 --> 00:17:50.103
The accessibility lead's role is to
keep those responsibilities connected

00:17:50.551 --> 00:17:53.997
and make sure that accessibility
doesn't disappear between all these

00:17:54.018 --> 00:17:56.689
roles, teams, or even organizations.

00:17:58.348 --> 00:18:01.396
The role doesn't require
a particular job title.

00:18:02.086 --> 00:18:04.716
Depending on the organization,
the accessibility lead could be

00:18:04.716 --> 00:18:08.725
a site owner, project manager,
product owner, accessibility

00:18:08.725 --> 00:18:12.485
specialist, or someone else who is
well-positioned to coordinate the work.

00:18:13.817 --> 00:18:17.841
They don't need to be the organization's
strongest accessibility expert either.

00:18:18.732 --> 00:18:23.153
But they do need enough understanding
to recognize accessibility risks and

00:18:23.154 --> 00:18:25.289
know when specialist input is needed.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:31.211
Alternatively, they need direct access
to people who can provide that expertise.

00:18:32.646 --> 00:18:38.463
But what the accessibility lead does need
is a clear mandate, and by mandate I mean

00:18:38.474 --> 00:18:42.598
enough authority to influence at least the
three things I mentioned earlier: design

00:18:42.598 --> 00:18:45.259
decisions, priorities, and processes.

00:18:46.473 --> 00:18:48.939
They need to be able to
raise accessibility concerns

00:18:48.957 --> 00:18:50.222
when decisions are made.

00:18:51.035 --> 00:18:54.526
They need to influence whether
accessibility work receives enough

00:18:54.527 --> 00:18:59.058
time and priority, and they need to
help make accessibility part of the

00:18:59.058 --> 00:19:03.259
normal processes instead of leaving
it to individual memory or goodwill

00:19:05.342 --> 00:19:09.139
The accessibility lead doesn't
necessarily make every final decision.

00:19:09.759 --> 00:19:14.670
Some decisions, such as accepting a known
risk, might still belong to the site owner

00:19:14.708 --> 00:19:16.702
or another authorized decision-maker.

00:19:17.608 --> 00:19:20.980
But the lead needs a clear route
into that decision-making process

00:19:21.185 --> 00:19:24.783
and enough authority to make sure
that accessibility is considered.

00:19:26.398 --> 00:19:30.266
The accessibility lead can also be
a group of people, especially when

00:19:30.268 --> 00:19:32.021
several organizations are involved.

00:19:32.141 --> 00:19:35.803
For example, the group might include
representatives from the site owner,

00:19:35.834 --> 00:19:37.909
development partner, content team.

00:19:38.533 --> 00:19:43.488
But even then, the group needs a
named coordinator, a clear way to make

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:47.980
decisions, and an escalation route,
'cause otherwise the group can become

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:50.324
yet another version of someone else.

00:19:51.946 --> 00:19:54.177
So the exact role can vary.

00:19:54.867 --> 00:19:58.732
What matters is that the accessibility
lead can keep the overall work connected,

00:19:59.157 --> 00:20:05.129
has access to the expertise they need,
and has a mandate to influence real change

00:20:07.479 --> 00:20:10.346
Now, naming the accessibility
lead might take some time.

00:20:10.958 --> 00:20:14.427
In the meantime, you can already start
to strengthen the system with a few

00:20:14.487 --> 00:20:16.110
lightweight governance practices.

00:20:16.819 --> 00:20:21.877
And by governance, I just mean agreed
roles, rules, checkpoints, all that,

00:20:21.892 --> 00:20:25.746
that stop accessibility from depending
on memory or individual goodwill.

00:20:27.492 --> 00:20:31.486
Because when we are first starting,
we shouldn't have a 200-page

00:20:31.513 --> 00:20:33.488
manual that will overwhelm people.

00:20:34.295 --> 00:20:38.484
But instead, we should have a
set of tools and processes to

00:20:38.484 --> 00:20:40.500
help us to do our job correctly.

00:20:41.448 --> 00:20:44.524
Ideally, we'll start building
accessibility governance

00:20:44.548 --> 00:20:46.372
into the existing workflows.

00:20:46.838 --> 00:20:49.741
That way, the change isn't
too big, and you might reduce

00:20:49.753 --> 00:20:51.123
some resistance to change.

00:20:51.957 --> 00:20:57.426
So it's important that we start somewhere
and then improve from there step by step

00:21:00.184 --> 00:21:03.973
The first practice is to have
a content publishing checklist.

00:21:04.812 --> 00:21:08.139
Even if the design and technical
implementation were fully accessible,

00:21:08.696 --> 00:21:12.476
if content isn't accessible,
the site isn't accessible.

00:21:13.591 --> 00:21:16.859
So you might want to have some different
checkpoints for accessibility, and

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:19.637
that all depends on the knowledge
level of the content editors.

00:21:20.185 --> 00:21:23.774
But here are a few common things
to check before publishing content.

00:21:24.624 --> 00:21:27.416
So use heading levels to
represent the logical content

00:21:27.416 --> 00:21:29.137
structure, not visual appearance.

00:21:30.408 --> 00:21:31.934
Make link text descriptive.

00:21:32.167 --> 00:21:34.975
Avoid the click here or
read more types of links.

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:41.030
Ensure sufficient color contrast, and
here, give content editors the approved

00:21:41.057 --> 00:21:45.714
color combinations or the exact contrast
thresholds they're expected to follow.

00:21:46.726 --> 00:21:50.640
Then, of course, write meaningful
alt text, and for media,

00:21:50.673 --> 00:21:53.783
provide the required captions,
transcripts, and audio description.

00:21:54.955 --> 00:21:58.629
And after a while, you might want
to add more things to the checklist,

00:21:58.672 --> 00:22:00.462
and that is very much okay.

00:22:01.393 --> 00:22:05.291
But remember to keep each
set of changes small.

00:22:05.751 --> 00:22:08.617
Make the shift as easy as
possible for the content editors.

00:22:09.143 --> 00:22:12.174
Now, having five items to check is doable.

00:22:12.175 --> 00:22:14.674
It doesn't feel like it will
complicate the work too much.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:19.581
But if the first checklist they
ever see has fifty items on it, they

00:22:19.581 --> 00:22:21.485
might get overloaded and shut down.

00:22:21.871 --> 00:22:24.981
It might feel like their
workload doubles or even triples.

00:22:25.488 --> 00:22:28.773
And we all know that people are
already very busy, so aim to

00:22:28.775 --> 00:22:30.392
make these changes manageable.

00:22:31.234 --> 00:22:37.005
Then we have the definition of done
and acceptance criteria that solve

00:22:37.043 --> 00:22:39.115
two related but different problems.

00:22:40.065 --> 00:22:44.344
So the definition of done contains
the recurring quality expectations

00:22:44.379 --> 00:22:46.187
that apply across relevant work.

00:22:46.985 --> 00:22:49.981
It might require appropriate
manual testing, reviewed

00:22:49.988 --> 00:22:53.325
automated results, documentation
of what was checked, and so on.

00:22:54.438 --> 00:22:58.778
Acceptance criteria describe what must
be true for this particular feature.

00:22:59.994 --> 00:23:04.423
For our form plugin example, those
criteria might say that the form

00:23:04.424 --> 00:23:08.051
can be completed with a keyboard,
focus remains visible, fields have

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:12.681
accessible names and instructions,
and error messages are identified and

00:23:12.681 --> 00:23:14.358
associated with the correct fields.

00:23:15.014 --> 00:23:20.213
And that relevant screen reader
behavior should also be checked So the

00:23:20.214 --> 00:23:25.279
definition of done keeps accessibility
in the normal workflow, and acceptance

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:29.997
criteria make the expected behavior
of this particular feature concrete.

00:23:31.713 --> 00:23:35.461
Imagine that the original ticket
only says, "Install a form

00:23:35.464 --> 00:23:36.914
plugin for the campaign page."

00:23:37.736 --> 00:23:41.218
There's no accessibility expectation,
so the team can complete the

00:23:41.218 --> 00:23:44.286
technical installation and
still consider the task done.

00:23:45.523 --> 00:23:47.908
A better ticket combines both levels.

00:23:48.729 --> 00:23:52.943
The definition of done requires the
relevant testing and evidence, and

00:23:52.943 --> 00:23:56.201
the acceptance criteria describe
the form's expected behavior, the

00:23:56.209 --> 00:23:59.568
keyboard support, visible focus,
accessible names, instructions,

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:01.098
and understandable error handling.

00:24:03.307 --> 00:24:07.400
That changes accessibility from
an assumption into something

00:24:07.858 --> 00:24:09.844
the team can actually verify.

00:24:10.406 --> 00:24:12.417
And third, we have plugins.

00:24:13.926 --> 00:24:17.672
Plugins can affect both the public
website and the WordPress dashboard,

00:24:18.347 --> 00:24:22.429
and if we only check the visitor-facing
interface, we might prevent disabled

00:24:22.431 --> 00:24:27.299
content editors from doing their work,
so don't forget the admin side either.

00:24:29.272 --> 00:24:33.854
So before installing a plugin, look
for accessibility documentation, known

00:24:33.855 --> 00:24:36.130
limitations, and configuration guidance.

00:24:36.885 --> 00:24:41.979
These don't prove that the plugin is
accessible, but they can help identify

00:24:42.018 --> 00:24:43.608
what actually needs to be tested.

00:24:45.111 --> 00:24:47.975
And test relevant user
journeys with a keyboard.

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:52.173
For interactive interfaces, include
an appropriate screen reader check.

00:24:52.854 --> 00:24:57.342
Review semantics such as native controls,
accessible names, dates, instructions,

00:24:57.393 --> 00:25:01.919
and then of course run an automated tool
to identify machine testable problems.

00:25:03.771 --> 00:25:08.295
So it's also important to test the
actual configuration, well, in staging

00:25:08.298 --> 00:25:12.785
hopefully, against the agreed acceptance
criteria, because a plugin might behave

00:25:12.802 --> 00:25:16.978
differently depending on its settings,
theme, and a surrounding implementation.

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:23.442
And well, sometimes none of the
available options is fully accessible.

00:25:24.158 --> 00:25:26.274
That doesn't make the decision disappear.

00:25:27.539 --> 00:25:32.243
So the development or maintenance
team should document what was tested,

00:25:32.423 --> 00:25:36.687
what works, what doesn't work, and
what alternatives were considered.

00:25:37.802 --> 00:25:42.533
The site owner or a delegated
decision-maker then has enough information

00:25:42.606 --> 00:25:48.573
des- to decide whether the plugin should
be rejected, replaced, customized, or

00:25:48.633 --> 00:25:53.406
accepted temporarily with documented
limitations and a remediation plan.

00:25:54.987 --> 00:25:57.081
That is an important part of ownership.

00:25:57.879 --> 00:26:01.820
The specialist provides evidence,
but someone with an appropriate

00:26:01.846 --> 00:26:05.697
mandate must decide how the
organization responds to the risk.

00:26:07.139 --> 00:26:11.288
And, well, everyone here probably already
knows this, but I'll state anyway,

00:26:12.032 --> 00:26:16.640
temporary acceptance shouldn't mean
quietly accepting the problem forever.

00:26:17.333 --> 00:26:21.683
It needs a named decision-maker,
documented impact, a reason for

00:26:21.683 --> 00:26:25.801
that decision, and a remediation
action, and a date for review.

00:26:26.562 --> 00:26:30.640
Because otherwise, these temporary
limitations have a bit of a

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:33.011
habit of becoming permanent

00:26:35.519 --> 00:26:37.897
And then fourth, handovers.

00:26:37.984 --> 00:26:39.502
So handovers happen all the time.

00:26:39.903 --> 00:26:43.188
Things move from the client to
design, from design to development,

00:26:43.198 --> 00:26:46.703
from development to the content
editors, and so on and so forth.

00:26:47.693 --> 00:26:52.763
It's important to have a clear protocol
for handovers because, well, remember the

00:26:52.774 --> 00:26:54.735
multi-agency blame game we talked about?

00:26:55.416 --> 00:26:56.729
This is where we can stop it.

00:26:57.591 --> 00:27:01.107
So your handover process shouldn't
just be an internal checklist.

00:27:01.462 --> 00:27:06.000
It should be a formal handover point
between organizations, not merely

00:27:06.002 --> 00:27:07.774
an informal exchange of files.

00:27:08.912 --> 00:27:12.809
But this applies to internal handovers
as well because, well, people change

00:27:12.821 --> 00:27:17.255
roles, team members leave, and
responsibilities move even when the

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:19.813
organization and vendor remain the same.

00:27:20.957 --> 00:27:24.316
So the handover should also state
who coordinates future accessibility

00:27:24.316 --> 00:27:28.500
work, who performs update checks,
and who is authorized to make

00:27:28.503 --> 00:27:30.207
decisions about known limitations.

00:27:31.717 --> 00:27:33.786
And what else should the handover contain?

00:27:34.794 --> 00:27:39.124
The most important part is to think
what the other party needs in order

00:27:39.126 --> 00:27:43.578
to do their job in an accessible
way and provide that information.

00:27:44.283 --> 00:27:46.330
This is the minimum you
should always provide.

00:27:47.017 --> 00:27:50.434
Any relevant requirements,
specifications, and agreed client

00:27:50.437 --> 00:27:54.501
needs should also be included because
they provide necessary context.

00:27:56.081 --> 00:28:00.545
For our form plugin example, the
handover should not just say which

00:28:00.576 --> 00:28:04.463
plugin was installed, but it should
explain why that plugin was chosen,

00:28:04.899 --> 00:28:08.194
which version and configuration were
tested, what accessibility checks were

00:28:08.201 --> 00:28:12.492
completed, and whether there are known
limitations, and who's responsible for

00:28:12.492 --> 00:28:14.197
future updates and regression testing.

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:18.831
And if the implementation includes
accessibility customizations,

00:28:18.896 --> 00:28:20.759
those also need to be documented.

00:28:21.404 --> 00:28:25.190
Otherwise, a future update or even
a new maintenance team might end

00:28:25.194 --> 00:28:28.658
up removing those without realizing
what they were actually protecting.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.466
So the next team needs to understand
the decision, the tested configuration,

00:28:35.812 --> 00:28:39.754
known limitations, and what must
be rechecked after an update.

00:28:40.904 --> 00:28:44.925
And if possible, record any
handover meetings for future use.

00:28:45.633 --> 00:28:48.967
Then the people will also have more
than just the written documentation.

00:28:49.993 --> 00:28:53.315
And consider the roles and
responsibilities of the other parties

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:57.841
and ensure you provide enough information
for everyone to do their job well.

00:28:59.156 --> 00:29:02.907
In my opinion, the biggest thing about
handovers is that you're supposed to

00:29:02.907 --> 00:29:05.483
make the other parties' lives easier.

00:29:05.811 --> 00:29:11.120
So don't gatekeep information, and
no matter the situation, always

00:29:11.125 --> 00:29:15.141
have a good and detailed handover
that aims to keep the site in good

00:29:15.142 --> 00:29:21.431
condition, no matter who is actively
maintaining it And last but not least,

00:29:21.853 --> 00:29:23.561
continuous accessibility monitoring.

00:29:24.388 --> 00:29:26.576
We should do regular accessibility checks.

00:29:27.711 --> 00:29:32.294
It doesn't always have to be a full-scale
accessibility audit, but even having

00:29:32.295 --> 00:29:35.765
a regular check with an automated or
semi-automated tool will help reduce

00:29:35.777 --> 00:29:37.250
some of the most common problems.

00:29:37.926 --> 00:29:41.670
Paid services such as WebAIM
or Siteimprove can monitor many

00:29:41.671 --> 00:29:46.170
pages across a site, while browser
plugins analyze one page at a time.

00:29:47.259 --> 00:29:51.727
And while automated tools cannot
catch everything, they can detect many

00:29:51.797 --> 00:29:56.695
recurring machine testable problems,
including several patterns highlighted

00:29:56.805 --> 00:29:59.593
each year by the WebAIM Million analysis.

00:30:00.405 --> 00:30:03.605
Let's do our part to start
improving those numbers.

00:30:05.089 --> 00:30:08.027
So all we need to do is
to use a tool, right?

00:30:08.483 --> 00:30:08.721
Nope.

00:30:09.555 --> 00:30:12.397
This is where the importance
of ownership shows up.

00:30:12.939 --> 00:30:18.089
It's not enough to just have a tool
available The accessibility lead makes

00:30:18.089 --> 00:30:22.280
sure that the monitoring process is
defined and connected to normal work.

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:27.240
They make sure the organization
has agreed answers to questions

00:30:27.292 --> 00:30:29.791
such as, who reviews the results?

00:30:30.334 --> 00:30:31.093
How often?

00:30:32.090 --> 00:30:33.776
Where are the findings recorded?

00:30:34.307 --> 00:30:35.664
And who prioritizes them?

00:30:36.557 --> 00:30:39.798
What response times apply
to high-impact issues?

00:30:40.819 --> 00:30:43.261
Who verifies that a fix actually worked?

00:30:43.712 --> 00:30:48.398
And where does accessibility feedback
from actual users enter the same process?

00:30:49.681 --> 00:30:54.048
So the lead doesn't have to necessarily
review every result, fix every issue,

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:59.070
but their role is to make sure that
these questions have agreed answers.

00:31:00.121 --> 00:31:02.550
And now back to our example form plugin.

00:31:02.985 --> 00:31:06.025
It's now finally live, but
the process doesn't end there.

00:31:06.478 --> 00:31:11.196
Because a plugin update might change its
markup or keyboard behavior, or a new

00:31:11.204 --> 00:31:13.264
form might be configured differently.

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:18.856
Monitoring might detect a machine-testable
problem, or a user might report that an

00:31:18.869 --> 00:31:20.896
error message is not announced correctly.

00:31:21.766 --> 00:31:25.893
Those findings need to enter the same
normal workflow as any other defects.

00:31:27.112 --> 00:31:28.913
So someone reviews the finding.

00:31:29.711 --> 00:31:33.451
The accessibility lead ensures it
reaches the agreed prioritization

00:31:33.461 --> 00:31:38.028
process, the responsible team fixes
it, and the relevant behavior is

00:31:38.028 --> 00:31:39.844
retested before the issue is closed.

00:31:41.003 --> 00:31:45.922
And if the same type of finding keeps
returning, that should trigger a change

00:31:45.923 --> 00:31:50.201
to the underlying process, template,
training, or the definition of done,

00:31:50.746 --> 00:31:52.630
not just another individual fix.

00:31:54.860 --> 00:31:58.327
Automated monitoring might notice
things like missing labels or certain

00:31:58.329 --> 00:32:00.229
in- certain invalid relationships.

00:32:00.363 --> 00:32:04.593
It will not necessarily tell us whether
the form instructions are understandable

00:32:05.183 --> 00:32:09.649
or whether the entire error recovery
experience actually works well.

00:32:10.344 --> 00:32:13.777
That's why targeted manual checks
are still needed, especially

00:32:13.777 --> 00:32:15.117
after significant updates.

00:32:16.184 --> 00:32:20.984
But with this workflow, the form plugin
is no longer an isolated technical choice.

00:32:21.378 --> 00:32:25.618
It has a clear decision-making, named
testing responsibilities, requirements,

00:32:25.681 --> 00:32:29.085
a release checkpoint, and documentation
and a route for future findings.

00:32:30.293 --> 00:32:32.499
So a lightweight routine
might look like this.

00:32:33.447 --> 00:32:35.990
Automated results are
reviewed every month.

00:32:36.511 --> 00:32:40.070
High-risk changes receive targeted
manual testing before release.

00:32:40.477 --> 00:32:43.997
And the overall accessibility situation
is reviewed a few times a year.

00:32:44.936 --> 00:32:48.355
User feedback enters the same
backlog as any monitoring findings.

00:32:49.274 --> 00:32:53.542
High-impact problems are escalated
quickly, while recurring lower-level

00:32:53.545 --> 00:32:56.255
problems might trigger changes
to the template, training,

00:32:56.255 --> 00:32:57.200
or the definition of done.

00:32:58.384 --> 00:33:02.145
The exact rhythm depends on the
site, but the important thing is

00:33:02.213 --> 00:33:06.582
that the rhythm, responsibilities,
and follow-up are agreed in advance

00:33:09.086 --> 00:33:10.274
Let's do a quick recap

00:33:12.318 --> 00:33:17.861
So accessibility can fail because
knowledge, tools, processes,

00:33:18.157 --> 00:33:20.593
communication, or ownership are missing.

00:33:21.532 --> 00:33:25.049
Clear ownership is what helps the
organization notice and address

00:33:25.074 --> 00:33:28.605
the other gaps and prevents the
same problem from returning.

00:33:30.241 --> 00:33:35.373
Accessibility doesn't usually fail
because people don't care or because

00:33:35.384 --> 00:33:37.593
they are deliberately doing poor work.

00:33:38.101 --> 00:33:42.310
It fails when the system around their
work doesn't support accessibility.

00:33:42.937 --> 00:33:46.839
So who owns accessibility after
a WordPress site launches?

00:33:47.288 --> 00:33:49.367
The answer has three parts.

00:33:51.074 --> 00:33:55.331
One, the organization operating
the service is ultimately

00:33:55.369 --> 00:33:56.805
accountable for the outcome.

00:33:58.365 --> 00:34:03.442
Two, designers, developers,
content editors, project managers,

00:34:03.523 --> 00:34:08.165
and partners are responsible for
accessibility in their own work.

00:34:09.446 --> 00:34:14.743
And three, a named accessibility lead
keeps the whole system connected.

00:34:16.118 --> 00:34:19.377
Ownership doesn't mean that
one person performs every

00:34:19.377 --> 00:34:21.239
check or fixes every problem.

00:34:21.288 --> 00:34:26.270
It means that accountability is clear,
responsibilities are assigned, and

00:34:26.295 --> 00:34:28.252
someone coordinates the whole thing

00:34:30.940 --> 00:34:35.536
So we have named the accessibility lead,
but just naming someone isn't enough.

00:34:36.098 --> 00:34:42.228
The role needs a mandate, clear authority
to influence how accessibility is handled.

00:34:43.038 --> 00:34:47.580
That means accessibility expectations
can become agreed rules rather

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:49.062
than optional suggestions.

00:34:50.058 --> 00:34:53.982
The lead can raise risks when
priorities and schedules are decided,

00:34:54.891 --> 00:34:58.274
and they can help build accessibility
into normal processes such as

00:34:58.308 --> 00:35:02.639
acceptance criteria, handovers,
release checkpoints, and monitoring.

00:35:03.799 --> 00:35:08.473
So they don't need to make every
final decision or perform every check

00:35:08.490 --> 00:35:14.114
personally, but they need a clear route
into decision-making and enough authority

00:35:14.115 --> 00:35:16.419
to ensure agreed actions move forward.

00:35:17.604 --> 00:35:20.569
Without that mandate, we have
created an advisory role.

00:35:21.299 --> 00:35:27.101
But with it, the accessibility lead can
connect decisions, priorities, processes,

00:35:27.212 --> 00:35:29.692
and monitoring to actual improvement.

00:35:30.877 --> 00:35:32.719
So where should you start tomorrow?

00:35:33.246 --> 00:35:37.502
Well, first begin the discussion about who
will fulfill the accessibility lead role.

00:35:38.473 --> 00:35:42.747
This might be one person or a
group of people, but the role

00:35:42.748 --> 00:35:47.500
needs a clear mandate to influence
priorities, decisions, and processes.

00:35:49.201 --> 00:35:51.969
Then choose one high-risk workflow.

00:35:52.759 --> 00:35:54.969
Plugin installation is a good example.

00:35:55.518 --> 00:35:59.248
So define the minimum criteria
that every new plugin must meet.

00:35:59.786 --> 00:36:03.634
Decide who verifies them in staging,
and name the person responsible

00:36:03.639 --> 00:36:05.297
for following up after release.

00:36:06.996 --> 00:36:12.423
Note, you don't need to rebuild
every accessibility process at once.

00:36:13.066 --> 00:36:18.688
One clearly owned workflow already
removes one place where someone else

00:36:18.799 --> 00:36:20.466
is currently who we are depending on.

00:36:22.343 --> 00:36:27.125
And once the coordination and authority
are clear, the systematic accessibility

00:36:27.134 --> 00:36:28.918
improvement can finally begin.

00:36:30.971 --> 00:36:37.029
So don't just launch an accessible
site, but build a system that actually

00:36:37.033 --> 00:36:42.459
keeps it accessible because that is
how accessibility is maintained instead

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:44.810
of gradually declining after launch.

00:36:46.958 --> 00:36:46.978
Thank you

00:36:54.014 --> 00:36:54.604
Awesome.

00:36:54.697 --> 00:36:55.794
Thank you so much.

00:36:55.826 --> 00:37:00.269
Um, I really love the call-out
about accessibility failing

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:04.919
because of a lack of ownership
and not necessarily about caring.

00:37:04.971 --> 00:37:09.355
I think that's something that, um, we talk
a lot about, is that you need to start

00:37:09.355 --> 00:37:13.429
caring, but then, you know, implementing
it is really where it falls, falls short.

00:37:13.975 --> 00:37:19.071
Um, we do have a couple of questions
from our, our, our viewers here.

00:37:19.357 --> 00:37:24.909
Um, the first one, uh: in your experience,
what are the biggest roadblocks for

00:37:24.909 --> 00:37:27.135
organizations getting on board with this?

00:37:27.373 --> 00:37:32.081
I can imagine a combination of,
you know, lacking internal systems,

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:36.735
staffing, support from upper
management, or just a lack of awareness.

00:37:36.747 --> 00:37:41.143
You know, what is, is, um, the
biggest roadblock in your experience?

00:37:42.785 --> 00:37:45.989
Yeah, all of these are pretty
much- Yeah … happening.

00:37:46.551 --> 00:37:51.307
Um, well, one thing that isn't mentioned
here, especially during these times where

00:37:51.308 --> 00:37:56.473
the market situation is a bit tricky,
of course money comes into play as well.

00:37:57.064 --> 00:38:02.327
So… But I think it's not just
money, it's, you know, money

00:38:02.328 --> 00:38:04.555
combined with lack of awareness.

00:38:05.405 --> 00:38:09.568
So at least I've received that, "You
know, we don't have the budget for

00:38:09.569 --> 00:38:14.003
that. We can't include this." I think
that is probably the most common

00:38:14.414 --> 00:38:17.155
block that I've, I've encountered.

00:38:17.925 --> 00:38:22.162
But yeah, it all… I think it all
starts by raising the awareness.

00:38:22.164 --> 00:38:26.725
But like I said, it's not all just
about caring, because that's what

00:38:26.726 --> 00:38:30.277
I thought in the beginning, that if
people just cared, things would be

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:34.551
fixed immediately, and that's not
true, in the business world at least.

00:38:34.551 --> 00:38:34.561
Yeah.

00:38:35.865 --> 00:38:43.298
So how might you recommend somebody
go about bringing this up to maybe

00:38:43.326 --> 00:38:47.541
a manager or, you know, a, you know,
the owner of a company in a way that

00:38:47.541 --> 00:38:51.818
doesn't then have that owner being like,
"Great, now you can then take care of

00:38:51.818 --> 00:38:55.400
all of it. You know, you're the person
who wants this, now you can do it.

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:56.914
S- we don't have the money for that."

00:38:56.929 --> 00:38:59.170
Is there some sort of maybe
strategy that you have?

00:39:01.185 --> 00:39:01.489
Yeah.

00:39:01.555 --> 00:39:03.891
Well, I don't have a perfect
answer for that, but- Yeah.

00:39:04.283 --> 00:39:09.373
… I think I would, whenever I approach
management, I would present them

00:39:09.374 --> 00:39:11.134
with as much data as possible.

00:39:11.186 --> 00:39:14.634
You know, what's the current situation?

00:39:15.012 --> 00:39:18.165
You know, are there currently delays
or something that they can put, you

00:39:18.165 --> 00:39:21.124
know, monetary effect on this problem?

00:39:21.851 --> 00:39:27.142
And then maybe I would just try to explain
why this cannot be a single person job.

00:39:28.157 --> 00:39:29.538
Mm. To, to present the case.

00:39:30.585 --> 00:39:36.076
It's still a risk that it will fall
on the, the person's hands, but I

00:39:36.079 --> 00:39:39.067
have a feeling also that the person
who is taking this forward might

00:39:39.080 --> 00:39:44.122
also be partially interested at least
in being part of that change, so.

00:39:44.495 --> 00:39:45.234
But yeah.

00:39:46.639 --> 00:39:52.894
Just present data and numbers to try to
avoid having to be the solo person there.

00:39:53.905 --> 00:39:55.012
Yeah, definitely.

00:39:55.269 --> 00:39:57.715
Um, I think that leads
into the next question.

00:39:57.798 --> 00:39:59.970
Um, it's sort of, I think, a two-parter.

00:39:59.974 --> 00:40:04.607
You sort of have, um, what are your
thoughts on the solo accessibility

00:40:04.608 --> 00:40:08.745
champion, you know, dedicated
accessibility team embedding

00:40:08.747 --> 00:40:11.313
accessibility as part of everyone's job?

00:40:11.587 --> 00:40:17.013
Um, do, does the way that you implement
that change how ongoing attention

00:40:17.264 --> 00:40:19.485
to accessibility can be implemented?

00:40:20.950 --> 00:40:22.745
Yeah, I think they'll
make a big difference.

00:40:22.842 --> 00:40:27.033
So of course it depends on how
those roles are defined, and the

00:40:27.034 --> 00:40:28.329
organization and all, all that.

00:40:28.332 --> 00:40:29.091
We all know that.

00:40:29.577 --> 00:40:33.998
But yeah, absolutely, because then
if you have at least, you know, the

00:40:34.083 --> 00:40:37.927
dedicated accessibility team in place,
that should mean that there's at

00:40:37.930 --> 00:40:39.853
least some processes already in place.

00:40:40.219 --> 00:40:43.902
So it might actually make it
easier to expand it a bit to

00:40:43.993 --> 00:40:45.230
view that accessibility lead.

00:40:45.253 --> 00:40:48.721
Of course, the dedicated
accessibility team could also be the

00:40:48.721 --> 00:40:51.094
accessibility lead in some cases.

00:40:51.959 --> 00:40:56.675
Um, and solo accessibility champions,
well, I've been that several times.

00:40:57.210 --> 00:41:03.067
So it, it also makes a difference of
course, but at least I would hope that

00:41:03.068 --> 00:41:07.279
organizations would start to see that
having just the one person, you know,

00:41:07.922 --> 00:41:11.799
yelling on the roof of that, "Hey, let's
make this happen," it's a burnout risk,

00:41:11.921 --> 00:41:14.312
of course, so that's, that's a risk there.

00:41:14.312 --> 00:41:14.322
Yeah.

00:41:14.322 --> 00:41:15.572
But yeah.

00:41:16.635 --> 00:41:21.397
So yeah, of course it depends on how those
roles are defined, but they absolutely

00:41:21.397 --> 00:41:25.007
do matter, and I would recommend
having those types of roles at least.

00:41:25.437 --> 00:41:28.238
Of course, it depends on the,
what the organization does.

00:41:28.599 --> 00:41:33.441
But for example, in, uh, my
employer's case, we have a

00:41:33.474 --> 00:41:34.855
two-person accessibility team.

00:41:35.795 --> 00:41:37.410
It's a bit of a flow.

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:38.640
We think that what are we actually doing?

00:41:38.641 --> 00:41:40.709
But we are doing the, you
know, accessibility audit

00:41:40.746 --> 00:41:41.935
work and this kind of stuff.

00:41:41.991 --> 00:41:46.115
But then we are also the two people
who are developers and designers can

00:41:46.142 --> 00:41:49.830
contact any time with any questions,
and that makes a big difference.

00:41:49.833 --> 00:41:50.672
Because before…

00:41:51.204 --> 00:41:53.901
Well, it's me and my colleague
who are in this role.

00:41:54.618 --> 00:42:00.847
Before we were there, people had
nowhere to ask, so they might ask on

00:42:00.848 --> 00:42:03.652
some public channel, and then someone
might answer or someone might not.

00:42:04.248 --> 00:42:08.729
So yeah, absolutely having any of
these in place is super important,

00:42:08.731 --> 00:42:13.095
and it can really help because maybe
that's the place to start, actually.

00:42:13.354 --> 00:42:16.827
Instead of going directly that we
need this accessibility lead, and we

00:42:16.827 --> 00:42:20.567
need to do this magnificent thing,
the first step could be that, hey,

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:26.810
should we have someone who is the, you
know, dedicated accessibility person?

00:42:27.234 --> 00:42:28.532
Uh, let's go from there.

00:42:29.361 --> 00:42:29.698
Yeah.

00:42:29.699 --> 00:42:36.441
That's a good, a good point, too, and
I do like the idea of, um, not having,

00:42:36.586 --> 00:42:42.241
always having the same person leading
every single thing, especially being able

00:42:42.242 --> 00:42:47.324
to have maybe a designer or a developer
or a project manager lead that, and

00:42:47.325 --> 00:42:51.252
then go to the accessibility people
as the resource, rather than having

00:42:51.252 --> 00:42:53.099
them also manage the whole process.

00:42:53.516 --> 00:42:54.161
So yeah, I like that.

00:42:54.161 --> 00:42:54.698
Yes, exactly.

00:42:55.331 --> 00:42:55.628
Yeah.

00:42:56.801 --> 00:42:58.472
Um, we do have a question about AI.

00:42:58.495 --> 00:43:01.536
I think that's a, a
popular topic nowadays.

00:43:01.576 --> 00:43:07.325
Um, do you see- It is … opportunities
for, you know, a smart usage of AI to help

00:43:07.358 --> 00:43:13.059
these organizations do accessibility well
when launching and maintaining a project?

00:43:13.059 --> 00:43:16.982
Are there any opportunities that
you might, um, sort of off the

00:43:16.982 --> 00:43:21.358
top of your head see as good,
good parts to use that for?

00:43:22.476 --> 00:43:27.567
Yeah, I think AI can be a great help,
of course, as long as we remember that

00:43:27.670 --> 00:43:34.324
it's not actually intelligent, that it,
the LLMs are probability based things.

00:43:34.688 --> 00:43:40.879
So but, you know, of course,
developers can ask for it to help,

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:42.914
you know, ideate, ask questions.

00:43:43.566 --> 00:43:46.196
Designers can ask,
everybody can ask it things.

00:43:46.222 --> 00:43:48.487
That's a very good place for it.

00:43:48.542 --> 00:43:50.023
Of course, we need to be critical.

00:43:51.310 --> 00:43:55.509
But yeah, when maintaining
and launching, why not?

00:43:55.562 --> 00:44:00.561
I think we just need to be really
careful not to trust the AI in

00:44:00.562 --> 00:44:04.437
what it says, because at least, uh,
based on some testing I've seen and

00:44:04.437 --> 00:44:11.326
done, uh, if we talk about code,
uh, AI can be really enthusiastic of

00:44:11.356 --> 00:44:14.313
adding ARIA even in the places- Yeah

00:44:14.313 --> 00:44:15.334
it's not really necessary.

00:44:16.087 --> 00:44:18.102
So yeah.

00:44:18.183 --> 00:44:18.628
Yeah.

00:44:18.628 --> 00:44:23.123
I would agree that it sounds like,
um, it would probably fit more into

00:44:23.124 --> 00:44:26.826
that process and procedure side of
things to help automate and, and

00:44:26.828 --> 00:44:31.505
relieve the people running the project
and being able to sort of, you know,

00:44:32.033 --> 00:44:35.475
tackle the actual accessibility
issues, and that seems like- Yeah

00:44:35.476 --> 00:44:35.731
the sort of-

00:44:37.769 --> 00:44:39.319
Yeah, I, I think that might be.

00:44:40.373 --> 00:44:43.964
And of course, like you
mentioned, automations are

00:44:43.964 --> 00:44:45.336
a really good thing to help.

00:44:45.403 --> 00:44:45.761
Yeah.

00:44:45.761 --> 00:44:49.177
But I wouldn't maybe trust AI,
at least at this stage, with

00:44:49.202 --> 00:44:50.873
that completely, at least.

00:44:51.262 --> 00:44:52.691
Always have a human- Yeah … review.

00:44:53.969 --> 00:44:56.498
Because AI is really good at-
Do you have- … convincing us.

00:44:57.772 --> 00:44:58.266
Yeah.

00:44:58.312 --> 00:44:59.201
Yeah, exactly.

00:44:59.587 --> 00:45:06.198
Um, do you have any kind of, um,
resources maybe, um, things that might

00:45:06.199 --> 00:45:11.178
